while it's brought some much-needed life  back to the box office after a  disappointing summer Darren Aronofsky's  mother is the first casualty of the fall  after opening up to just 7.5 million  dollars the harder to find film stars  Jennifer Lawrence as the title character  wife to Javier Bardem him as a woman who  must deal with the increasing stress of  unwanted visitors entering her  once-peaceful home the film's vague  logline has been a topic of discussion  for months and the movie became even  more shrouded in mystery as confusing  trailers and TV spots at the airwaves  while mother was as hard to track as it  was to explain no one expected the film  to fall as far as it did let's take a  look at why mother has had trouble  finding an audience critical division  while the Rotten Tomatoes score on  mother isn't terrible critics were  certainly divided over how good or bad  mother is some reviewers hailed the film  for its visual brilliance while others  were left confused and frustrated  calling the pic pretentious and  nonsensical yeah I don't like that  people who I'm not a masochist but I  like I want a reaction from people  although Aronofsky's movies have been  widely respected even if his best works  are hard to watch twice mother's reviews  indicated it might be hard to watch even  once some accuse the writer director of  trying to mix in too much allegory and  extremism to the film to endure others  thought it was bleak just for the sake  of it and almost everyone agreed that a  screening of mother is an intense  bewildering and disturbing experience  moviegoers obviously enjoyed the light  thrill of seeing a movie like it's where  the scares are comparatively wholesome  but audiences obviously weren't as  interested in traveling down another of  Aronofsky's existential wormholes this  weekend and even those who did see it  were unimpressed it was the opening  night audience that earned the film the  rare distinction of an F rating on  cinema score mother isn't the first film  from a director to get the dreaded  rating Steven Soderbergh Solaris also  holds that distinction so some consider  it a badge of honor but it's still  surprising to see a film with the  director and actors of this caliber be  so thoroughly docked by audiences mixed  marketing given its difficult premise  almost no one goes into mother really  knowing what it's about in advance part  of the reason for that is reviewers have  had such a tough time explaining what  happen  even after seeing the movie and despite  google searches for explanations spiking  after the film's release the results for  such queries can be just as confusing as  the movie itself audiences may often  enjoy twisty films but the biggest  problem with mother seems to be that no  one can agree on the true meaning is  this all just a cautionary tale on  climate change is the title character  really just mother earth who is being  abused by Javier Bardem God devil hybrid  and all the terrible people that flocked  to him or is the parable about the  Bible's creation story what Jennifer  Lawrence's character as some kind of Eve  no one can be quite sure we are all  living on this one planet with one  mother who gave us life given that  Aronofsky's last movie Noah was based on  the Bible and did well in the  international box office it might have  seemed like a good idea to continue on  the theological train but the fact that  no one's quite sure what mother is  trying to represent makes it a very  difficult sell bait-and-switch while  Jennifer Lawrence's fans might have been  shocked by seeing her ditched that  defiant persona that has informed so  much of her career so far general  audiences were probably more surprised  by how little horror the movie contains  the original teasers her mother made the  film seem like a horror movie which is  what earned some serious interest prior  to release the film's trailer drew out  some of the film's biggest scares using  the sounds of a ticking clock to  underscore what seemed to be a  psychologically focused haunted house  movie even Aronofsky himself insisted it  was meant to be a scare it began very  much with wanting to return to the  horror genre and did in Black Swan  however in the end that genre  identification effort backfired because  mother is definitely not your usual  Halloween fare instead the movie delves  into the human psyche albeit with some  very freaky imagery while those who are  familiar with Aronofsky's prior work  might not have been surprised by that  those who were just following the cues  left in the film's marketing were likely  fairly turned off by what they saw stiff  competition choosing the perfect release  date can be all-important to a film  success and studios will occasionally  shift around titles based on what other  companies are scheduling this was the  case with mother which saw its planned  October release moved up a month to  avoid competing with happy deathday and  while that might have saved them from  going up against that movie's crowd it  did end up hitting the  pick against Stephen King's it which  defied all expectations at the box  office and continued to draw in  audiences for its second weekend rather  than trying out something they didn't  understand and no one seemed to like  moviegoers seemed to take the safe bet  for their weekend thrills by hitting up  it which had a lot more word-of-mouth  buzz wide release washout despite the  big stars attached mother is ultimately  in arthouse film and one that really  isn't designed for a wide audience  Paramount Pictures might have been  looking at the potential dollar signs  when putting mother out to more than  2,300 theaters right away but in  hindsight it might have fared better by  seeking a limited release first the  strategy to put a niche film on a  limited theatrical run first before  expanding was exactly what worked for  Aronofsky's Black Swan which was  similarly eerie and stirred up its fair  share of audience confusion at the end -  but because of the cultural must-see  moment that was produced by Natalie  Portman's performance in the pic enough  buzz justified getting it into more  theaters and the movie turned a nice  profit as a result perhaps a slower  rollout could have saved mother from  being lashed at by audiences right away  to kill any momentum the movie might  have earned by creating such a  conversation among critics thanks for  watching click the looper icon to  subscribe to our YouTube channel plus  check out all this cool stuff we know  you'll love to  [Music]
 

